---
/**
 * @file qr/[slug].astro
 * @description The public landing for a printed QR code (HOS-981). Resolves the
 * slug against the API — which records the scan in the same call — and 302s to
 * the code's current target.
 *
 * @route /qr/{slug}/
 * @rendering SSR (on-demand), never cached
 *
 * ## Why this page is NOT under `/{lang}/`
 *
 * A QR code is printed on a physical sign. Its URL is set in ink and cannot be
 * changed afterwards — that indirection is the entire product. Putting a locale
 * in it would mean the print run chose, permanently, which language every
 * future scanner reads the site in. `isLanguageNeutralRoute` in the middleware
 * is what stops the locale redirect from touching it.
 *
 * "Touching", not "adding a prefix": `qr` is two letters, so it matches
 * `LOCALE_SHAPED_SEGMENT` and the middleware would read it as a request for an
 * unsupported language, REPLACING the segment rather than keeping it. Dropping
 * the exemption sends `/qr/Live2345/` to `/es/Live2345/` — a 404 — not to
 * `/es/qr/Live2345/`. This route depends on that exemption for existence, not
 * for speed.
 *
 * ## Why it is never cached
 *
 * Two independent reasons, either of which is sufficient: a cached response is
 * a scan that was never counted, and the target is editable at any moment, so a
 * cached one keeps sending people to a URL the operator has already replaced.
 * `applyCacheHeaders` is called with `cacheable: false` rather than a bare
 * header so the demotion goes through the same fail-closed path every other
 * page uses.
 *
 * ## Status codes
 *
 * 302 to the target when the slug resolves; 404 otherwise, with no attempt to
 * tell the reasons apart — the API already answers one identical body for
 * unknown, retired, deleted and malformed slugs, and re-deriving a distinction
 * here would re-open the oracle that design closes. The bare
 * `new Response(null, { status: 404 })` is the established shape: the
 * middleware rewrites it to the real site 404 page, so somebody who scanned a
 * dead sticker gets a page telling them the code no longer works instead of a
 * blank body or a silent bounce to the home page.
 */

import { qrApi } from '@/lib/api/endpoints';
import { applyCacheHeaders } from '@/lib/cache/response-cache';
import { CACHE_TAG_ALL } from '@repo/cache-tags';

const slug = Astro.params.slug as string;

// Never cacheable, so the tag list is inert — `applyCacheHeaders` returns
// before it reads `tags`. `CACHE_TAG_ALL` is filler for the non-empty tuple
// type, not a claim that anything purges this response.
applyCacheHeaders({
    locals: Astro.locals,
    headers: Astro.response.headers,
    cacheable: false,
    cacheClass: 'detail',
    tags: [CACHE_TAG_ALL]
});

// HOS-1141: the scan row records what the SCANNER's client is, and this page
// calls the API server-to-server. Without forwarding these headers the API
// would only ever see this web server's own `fetch`, and every row in
// `qr_code_scans` would describe the same machine — a column that is present,
// populated and wrong, which is worse than an absent one.
//
// `cookie` is forwarded so the API can fill `user_id`, and it is the COOKIE
// rather than a user id computed here for a reason worth keeping: this page
// never resolves a session of its own (`/qr/` is language-neutral and is not in
// `SESSION_OPTIONAL_SEGMENTS`, so the middleware sets `locals.user = null`
// without any auth round-trip). Forwarding moves the identification to the side
// that can VALIDATE it and costs this page nothing; computing an id here would
// first require adding the auth call this route deliberately does not make.
//
// KNOWN COST, stated rather than buried, and corrected after review — the first
// version of this note was optimistic on two counts.
//
// 1. "An anonymous scan sends no cookie" is FALSE. The site sets non-auth
//    cookies at `path=/`: the announcements dismissal
//    (`GlobalAnnouncements.astro:154`) and the consent banner
//    (`cookie-consent.ts:124`, `Path=/`). Anyone who has accepted the banner
//    forwards a `Cookie` header on this request while signed out. So the
//    forwarding is NOT limited to logged-in scanners.
// 2. The added cost is smaller than it first looked. `auth.api.getSession()`
//    already ran unconditionally on every API request before this PR, so a
//    cookie that carries no session changes nothing. What is genuinely NEW is
//    the uncached `user_role` query, and only for a cookie that resolves to a
//    real session.
//
// No third-party exposure: `request()` builds its URL as
// `${getBaseUrl()}${path}`, so the header only ever reaches our own API.
//
// And the failure mode this used to create is closed: `actorMiddleware` now
// degrades to a guest actor on this path instead of answering 503
// (`ACTOR_OPTIONAL_PATH_PATTERNS`), so a broken role read costs the `user_id`
// column and nothing else.
const result = await qrApi.resolve({
    slug,
    userAgent: Astro.request.headers.get('user-agent'),
    acceptLanguage: Astro.request.headers.get('accept-language'),
    cookieHeader: Astro.request.headers.get('cookie')
});

// `targetUrl` is checked, not just `data`. `apiClient.get` unwraps the API
// envelope WITHOUT validating it against a schema, so a 200 carrying a body
// that lacks the field yields a truthy `data` and an `undefined` target — which
// `Headers.set` would stringify into the literal `Location: "undefined"`,
// sending a scanner to `/qr/undefined`. A missing target is a code that does
// not resolve, and it takes the same 404 as every other such case.
if (!result.ok || !result.data?.targetUrl) {
    return new Response(null, { status: 404 });
}

// 302 and not 301: the target is editable, and a permanently-cached redirect in
// somebody's browser would outlive the operator's next edit with nothing able to
// evict it.
//
// The response is built by hand rather than with `Astro.redirect` because that
// helper constructs its own `Response` carrying only `Location` — the
// `Cache-Control` written onto `Astro.response.headers` above would not survive
// it, and an accidentally shared-cacheable redirect is exactly the failure this
// page is written to avoid. Copying the headers keeps the demotion (and
// anything else the pipeline attached) on the response that actually ships.
const redirectHeaders = new Headers(Astro.response.headers);
redirectHeaders.set('Location', result.data.targetUrl);

return new Response(null, { status: 302, headers: redirectHeaders });
---
